# Security Rules

All code in this project must follow security best practices. This file summarizes the key security requirements.

## Security Baselines

### S0 - Base Security (Always Apply)

- **Authentication**: Explicit strategy, enforced on every endpoint
- **Authorization**: Least privilege, deny by default
- **Input Validation**: Centralized, server-side validation
- **Output Encoding**: Context-appropriate encoding
- **Secrets**: Never in code or logs, loaded from secure storage
- **Transport**: HTTPS/TLS everywhere
- **Errors**: Generic messages to clients, details server-side only
- **Dependencies**: Locked versions, vulnerability scanning
- **Logging**: Structured logs with correlation IDs, no sensitive data
- **Rate Limiting**: On all public interfaces

### A0 - AI/LLM Security (When Building AI Features)

- **Untrusted Output**: Treat all model output as untrusted
- **Prompt Injection**: Sanitize inputs, separate system/user prompts
- **Tool Safety**: Allowlists, parameter validation, least privilege
- **Data Boundaries**: Tenant-scoped retrieval, role-based access
- **Output Controls**: Redaction, content filtering, human-in-the-loop
- **Cost Controls**: Token limits, rate limiting, timeouts
- **Audit**: Log AI actions without sensitive prompt details

### V0 - Audit/Evidence (When Compliance Required)

- **Event Logging**: Append-only, who/what/when, trace IDs
- **Tamper Evidence**: Hash chains where appropriate
- **Retention**: Defined policy, secure storage

### C0 - Cloud Deployment (When Deploying to Cloud)

- **IAM**: Least privilege roles, no long-lived credentials
- **Keys**: KMS/HSM storage, rotation strategy
- **Network**: Private networking, no public admin ports
- **Logging**: Central logging, security event alerts

## OWASP Top 10 Quick Reference

When reviewing or implementing, check against:

| Code | Risk | Key Control |
|------|------|-------------|
| A01 | Broken Access Control | Enforce authz on every request |
| A02 | Cryptographic Failures | Encrypt sensitive data, use TLS |
| A03 | Injection | Parameterized queries, input validation |
| A04 | Insecure Design | Threat modeling, secure patterns |
| A05 | Security Misconfiguration | Secure defaults, no debug in prod |
| A06 | Vulnerable Components | Dependency scanning, updates |
| A07 | Auth Failures | Strong auth, session management |
| A08 | Integrity Failures | Verify signatures, secure CI/CD |
| A09 | Logging Failures | Security event logging, monitoring |
| A10 | SSRF | Validate outbound URLs |

## OWASP LLM Top 10 Quick Reference

For AI/LLM features, also check:

| Code | Risk | Key Control |
|------|------|-------------|
| LLM01 | Prompt Injection | Input sanitization, separation |
| LLM02 | Insecure Output | Output validation, sandboxing |
| LLM03 | Training Poisoning | Data provenance, validation |
| LLM04 | Model DoS | Rate limits, token caps, timeouts |
| LLM05 | Supply Chain | Model/plugin verification |
| LLM06 | Info Disclosure | Data boundaries, redaction |
| LLM07 | Insecure Plugins | Allowlists, validation |
| LLM08 | Excessive Agency | Least privilege, human approval |
| LLM09 | Overreliance | Confidence checks, human oversight |
| LLM10 | Model Theft | Access controls, monitoring |

## Security in Change Documents

Every change document must include:

```markdown
## Security Impact
- **Risks reduced**: [e.g., A03 - adding parameterized queries]
- **New risks introduced**: [e.g., None, or LLM04 - new LLM endpoint]
- **OWASP mapping**: [e.g., A03-Injection, LLM01-Prompt Injection]
- **Controls implemented**: [e.g., #11, #82 from catalog]
```

## Security Review Checklist

Before approving any change:

- [ ] Input validation in place (controls 1-10)
- [ ] Output encoding appropriate (controls 11-18)
- [ ] Authentication verified (controls 19-26)
- [ ] Authorization enforced (controls 33-38)
- [ ] Secrets handled securely (control 44)
- [ ] Error handling safe (controls 45-50)
- [ ] Dependencies reviewed (controls 74-77)
- [ ] AI controls if applicable (controls 82-87)

